🚨 What is an Exception in Java?
An exception is an event that disrupts the normal flow of a program.
It occurs during runtime and can be caught and handled to prevent crashes.

✅ Why use Exception Handling?
To gracefully handle errors (e.g., file not found, divide by zero)

To prevent program crashes

To debug and log problems

To separate error-handling logic from business logic

🧩 Types of Exceptions in Java
Java exceptions fall under the Throwable class:

php
Copy
Edit
Throwable
├── Error (serious issues, not handled)
└── Exception
    ├── Checked Exception
    └── Unchecked Exception (RuntimeException)
1️⃣ Checked Exceptions (Compile-Time)
Checked by the compiler — must be declared with throws or handled with try-catch.

Typically for external issues like I/O, database, or network.

✅ Examples:

java
Copy
Edit
FileReader file = new FileReader("abc.txt"); // FileNotFoundException
Thread.sleep(1000); // InterruptedException
Common Checked Exceptions:

IOException

SQLException

FileNotFoundException

ClassNotFoundException

InterruptedException

2️⃣ Unchecked Exceptions (Runtime)
Subclass of RuntimeException

Not checked at compile time — developer’s responsibility to handle.

Usually caused by bad code or logic errors.

✅ Examples:

java
Copy
Edit
int x = 10 / 0;                  // ArithmeticException
String s = null; s.length();     // NullPointerException
int[] a = new int[2]; a[5] = 99; // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
Common Unchecked Exceptions:

NullPointerException

ArithmeticException

ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

IllegalArgumentException

ClassCastException

🔁 Other Types
3️⃣ Custom Exceptions
You can create your own exception class:

java
Copy
Edit
class MyException extends Exception {
    public MyException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
4️⃣ Errors
Serious issues like memory errors or JVM crashes.

Should not be handled by application code.

Examples:

OutOfMemoryError

StackOverflowError

🧪 Example: Handling Exception
java
Copy
Edit
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}
